WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.470
If you thought the stunts in the first six
Fast &amp; Furious movies were totally and utterly

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:11.289
insane, just wait till you see Furious 7 which
turns the insanity level up to 11 with a shed-load

00:00:11.289 --> 00:00:16.460
of gravity-defying, jaw-dropping action.
Yes, the Furious family is back for a tale

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:21.460
of revenge and terrorism that finds them continuing
the series’ jet-setting ways, leaping from

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.669
London to LA, Abu Dhabi, Tokyo, the Dominican
Republic, and Azerbaijan.

00:00:26.669 --> 00:00:31.449
As you know, sadly Paul Walker, who plays
Brian O’Conner, died in a car crash when

00:00:31.449 --> 00:00:37.250
he was part-way through filming Furious 7.
After that, the script was rewritten and Walker’s

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:42.490
brothers Caleb and Cody acted as stand-ins.
Given the circumstances, I can only imagine

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:45.460
how difficult it must have been for them to
do that.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:51.370
But thanks to them as well as some CGI jiggery-pokery
and some cunning camera positioning, Walker’s

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:55.830
part in the film was able to be completed.
On a couple of occasions, if you’re looking

00:00:55.830 --> 00:01:01.540
really closely, you may notice the CGI wizardry.
We’re talking seconds’ worth of footage

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.869
here, and I certainly never found it off-putting.
Given Walker’s untimely death, there are

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:09.509
a few moments in the movie that feel a touch
eerie.

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:14.570
But I’m not going to spoil anything by saying
what happens with Brian, suffice to say it

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:18.960
feels like a suitable send-off for both the
character and one of the franchise’s biggest

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.469
stars.
Don’t be surprised if you feel a tear or

00:01:22.469 --> 00:01:26.539
two rolling down your cheek by the end of
the movie; I know I did.

00:01:26.539 --> 00:01:31.909
For obvious reasons, Furious 7 feels like
the end of a chapter, if not of the franchise

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:36.310
itself, which I think they’ve left open
so they can gage what fan reaction would be

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:40.350
to a possible eighth movie.
There are plenty of call-backs to earlier

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:44.509
outings in the series, with characters, events,
dialogue, and even some product placement

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:47.950
popping up to firmly tie all seven movies
together.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:53.789
Alongside Furious mainstays Vin Diesel, Paul
Walker, Michelle Rodriguez, Tyrese Gibson,

00:01:53.789 --> 00:01:58.740
Jordana Brewster, and Ludacris, Dwayne Johnson,
the man who blew the cobwebs out of the franchise

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:03.359
in Fast Five, also returns.
And while we don’t get to see Johnson quite

00:02:03.359 --> 00:02:08.740
enough in Furious 7, he gets some brilliantly
ludicrous lines which he delivers as only

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:09.739
he can.

00:02:09.739 --> 00:02:13.349
There are also several new additions to life
in the Furious fast-lane.

00:02:13.349 --> 00:02:19.060
With echoes of Die Hard With A Vengeance,
Jason Statham turns up as the vengeful brother

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:24.420
of Luke Evans’ dastardly character, Owen
Shaw, who went up against Team Toretto in

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.790
Fast &amp; Furious 6.
Statham gets to make a very cool entrance

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:33.950
and has some great action scenes, including
a glass-shattering fight with Dwayne Johnson.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.079
But Statham’s not the only bad guy in the
movie.

00:02:37.079 --> 00:02:41.400
Djimon Hounsou, who you’ll remember from
Guardians Of The Galaxy, is drafted in to

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.860
play a random international terrorist who’s
hell bent on getting his hands on a super-tracking

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.750
device for reasons that are never made even
vaguely clear.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:55.540
Given his character has no back story and
relatively little screen time, Hounsou’s

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.870
basically wasted in this role.
In fact, I’m not sure why Statham’s character

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:04.349
couldn’t have filled both the avenging brother
AND the international terrorist roles in the

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:09.420
movie, which would also have helped simplify
the plot which does get a bit convoluted.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.319
Nathalie Emmanuel, best known as Game Of Thrones’
Missandei, makes her Fast &amp; Furious mark as

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:18.750
a British hacker who created the tracking
device that Hounsou’s baddie is after.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:23.689
Thai and mixed martial artists-turned-actors
Tony Jaa and Ronda Rousey get some kick-ass

00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:28.799
action, and Kurt Russell joins the cast as
a craft-beer-loving, shadowy man-in-black

00:03:28.799 --> 00:03:29.099
type.

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:33.579
For me, the Furious movies are at their best
when they skate in under the 2-hour mark,

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:38.480
at maybe 1 hour 45.
Furious 7 runs for nearly 2 hours 20, which

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.530
does start to feel a little on the long side
as the movie hits its final stretch, yet there’s

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:49.019
still plenty of blistering action in the film’s
final showdown, even if that finale does have

00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:51.129
a bit too much going on at once.

00:03:51.129 --> 00:03:57.530
In fact, Furious 7 has bucket-loads of what
this franchise does best: explosive, big-ass

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:03.109
action set-pieces which are in-freaking-sane!
Obviously, Newton didn’t bank on the Fast

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:07.540
&amp; Furious movies when he came up with his
laws of physics but these guys definitely

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:13.349
know a thing or two about defying gravity.
And while Furious 7 is a world away from Chitty

00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:18.709
Chitty Bang Bang, the Fast &amp; Furious crew
realise that that’s no reason to deny ourselves

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:23.810
stunning scenes of cars flying through the
air, one of which has shades of Die Hard 4

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.940
about it.
We also get to see cars smashing through skyscrapers

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:32.750
in a scene that calls to mind both Die Hard
and Mission Impossible: Ghost Protocol.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:37.350
And there’s the most awe-inspiring
bus-over-a-cliff-edge scene since The Italian

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:41.100
Job.
Essentially, Furious 7 is the Fast &amp; Furious

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:46.840
films on steroids, and if you're a petrolhead,
get ready for some serious car porn!

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:53.840
So, to sum up: Fast &amp; Furious 7 is a jaw-droppingly
insane, gravity-defying, nitrous-oxide-charged

00:04:54.930 --> 00:05:01.610
ride that manages to unite all seven movies
in the franchise while also delivering a tear-inducing

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:08.419
send-off to one of its family.
Fast &amp; Furious 7 gets 3.5 out of 5 Flicks.

00:05:08.419 --> 00:05:13.400
How excited are you for Furious 7? And if
you’ve seen it, what did you think?

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.180
Which is your favourite movie in the series?
And would you like to see more Fast &amp; Furious

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:22.250
movies after this, or is number 7 the right
spot to park the series?

